Add a featured discovery area, a rotating science fact, interactive challenge of the day, recently explored topics, popular simulations and a search bar labelled “What do you want to discover today?”

GLOBAL NAVIGATION:
Create a clean navigation bar containing Home, Discover, Virtual Lab, Physics Lab, Space Explorer, Rocket Lab, Energy Lab, Science Challenges and My Learning. Group related subjects under Discover when necessary to avoid clutter. Provide an obvious search function and a consistent way to return home.

THE INTERACTIVE CHEMISTRY LAB:
Build a dedicated virtual chemistry laboratory where users can learn about elements, molecules, chemical reactions and how substances behave when combined.

Include an interactive periodic table with all 118 elements. Each element should open a detailed panel containing its name, symbol, atomic number, relative atomic mass, group, period, electron configuration where supported, physical state at standard conditions, general properties, common uses, and relevant safety information. Distinguish naturally occurring elements from synthetic elements where appropriate.

Allow users to explore atoms and molecules using animated diagrams. Visualise protons, neutrons and electrons, explain atomic structure, show electron shells, demonstrate ionic and covalent bonding, and let users rotate or inspect simplified molecular models. Clearly label simplified scientific visualisations.

CHEMICAL MIXING SIMULATOR:
Create a virtual chemical reaction workbench. Users should select substances from a searchable catalogue, place them into virtual beakers or reaction chambers, adjust permitted quantities and conditions, and press a clearly labelled “Mix in Virtual Lab” button.

The simulator should show an animated, scientifically informed representation of what happens when substances are combined. Depending on the selected substances and the model, display:
- Whether a reaction is expected under the specified conditions.
- The difference between a chemical reaction and a physical mixture.
- Reactants and products.
- Balanced chemical equations when available.
- Changes in colour, temperature, pH, state or appearance when supported by evidence.
- Gas formation, precipitates, dissolving, crystallisation or other relevant observations.
- Energy absorbed or released, including an explanation of endothermic and exothermic reactions.
- The scientific explanation at particle and molecular levels.
- Reaction conditions and important limitations.
- Safety information, incompatibilities and hazards.
- A clear explanation of which observations are simulated and which properties are calculated or experimentally established.

Give users an expandable “What is happening at particle level?” section showing animated molecules before, during and after the reaction. Include a “Why did this happen?” explanation and a “Learn the science” button for deeper information.

Start with a curated library of safe, well-documented educational examples, such as vinegar and bicarbonate of soda, dissolving salt in water, dissolving sugar in water, and the behaviour of water and ice. Demonstrate that some combinations react while others simply dissolve, mix or remain separate. Explain that not all combinations produce a reaction.

Do not pretend to calculate unknown chemical reactions reliably. If a reaction is not supported by verified reference data, clearly explain that the result is unknown or not modelled. Do not invent products, equations, colours or reaction measurements.

Include a virtual-only simulation warning. Do not provide instructions for dangerous chemical mixtures, toxic gas generation, explosives, incendiary mixtures or other hazardous reactions. Flag incompatible substances and block unsafe combinations in the simulator. Do not encourage users to reproduce virtual results at home. Clearly distinguish educational simulation from practical laboratory work.

CHEMISTRY LEARNING MODULES:
Include interactive lessons on acids and bases, pH, indicators, the periodic table, chemical bonds, ionic compounds, covalent compounds, balancing equations, conservation of mass, oxidation and reduction, corrosion, catalysts, reaction rates, equilibrium, solutions, solubility, concentration, crystallisation, polymers, metals, non-metals, electrochemistry and organic chemistry.

Create a virtual pH scale from 0 to 14, with an explanation of acidity, neutrality and alkalinity. Use documented example substances and clearly explain that actual pH depends on concentration and conditions.

Allow learners to practise balancing chemical equations, identify reactants and products, build simple molecules and explore how temperature, concentration, surface area and catalysts may affect reaction rates. Use carefully labelled simplified models where real chemistry is too complex for a simple simulation.

THE PHYSICS AND SPEED LAB:
Create an interactive physics laboratory centred on motion, speed, acceleration, forces, momentum, energy and collisions.

Build a working speed simulator with a track, movable objects, cars, bicycles, spacecraft and other selectable objects. Let users change the distance travelled, elapsed time, mass, velocity and acceleration where appropriate. Show animated movement alongside numerical measurements and live graphs.

Include:
- Speed = distance ÷ time.
- Velocity and the difference between speed and velocity.
- Average and instantaneous speed.
- Acceleration and deceleration.
- Distance-time graphs.
- Velocity-time graphs.
- Acceleration-time graphs.
- Newton’s three laws of motion.
- Forces, friction, air resistance and drag.
- Momentum and collisions.
- Kinetic and potential energy.
- Work, power and mechanical efficiency.
- Terminal velocity and falling objects.
- Circular motion and centripetal acceleration.
- Relative motion and reference frames.

Let users compare the speed of a walking person, a bicycle, a car, a high-speed train, a jet aircraft, a rocket and light. Keep the units clear and allow conversion between metres per second, kilometres per hour and miles per hour. Use scientifically sensible values and explain when figures are approximate.

Add a custom experiment mode where users change variables and observe how the outcome changes. Provide a reset button, pause and resume controls, adjustable simulation speed and a step-by-step explanation.

THE WATER AND FLUID DYNAMICS LAB:
Create a visually engaging laboratory dedicated to water, fluids, liquids, pressure, waves, flow and buoyancy.

Include interactive simulations for:
- The water cycle, from evaporation to condensation and precipitation.
- Ice melting, water freezing and evaporation.
- Water density and temperature.
- Floating and sinking objects.
- Archimedes’ principle and buoyancy.
- Water pressure at different depths.
- Communicating vessels.
- Surface tension and capillary action.
- Waves travelling across water.
- Laminar and turbulent flow.
- Pipes, pumps and fluid resistance.
- Bernoulli’s principle.
- Waterfalls and gravitational potential energy.
- Dams and hydroelectric power.
- Tides and the influence of the Moon.
- Ocean currents and heat transfer.
- Flooding, water conservation and the global water supply.

Create a virtual tank where users can alter water depth and insert objects with different densities. Show whether each object floats, sinks or reaches neutral buoyancy, explaining why. Include a fluid flow experiment where users modify the shape of a pipe and compare flow speeds and pressure using a simplified model.

Show water molecules as animated particles and explain the unusual properties of water, including hydrogen bonding, its relatively high specific heat capacity and why ice is less dense than liquid water under ordinary conditions.

THE WIND, WEATHER AND ATMOSPHERE LAB:
Create an interactive weather and atmospheric science section. Use animated wind particles, air pressure maps, cloud formation and moving weather systems.

Allow users to adjust temperature differences, pressure gradients and surface conditions in simplified models to observe how air moves.

Include:
- What wind is and why it forms.
- Air pressure and pressure gradients.
- Convection and heat transfer.
- Sea breezes and land breezes.
- Global circulation and jet streams.
- Coriolis effects and rotating systems.
- Clouds, humidity and condensation.
- Rain, snow, hail and other precipitation.
- Thunderstorms, lightning and atmospheric electricity.
- Tornadoes and tropical cyclones.
- Hurricanes and storm development.
- Atmospheric layers and the ozone layer.
- Greenhouse gases and the greenhouse effect.
- Climate, weather and climate change.
- Wind turbines and renewable energy.
- The relationship between wind, drag and flight.

Add an interactive wind tunnel where users select the shape of an object and change air speed. Display qualitative or appropriately calculated airflow, pressure and drag. Explain that computational fluid dynamics is complex and that any simplified airflow visualisation has limitations.

