- The search for extraterrestrial life and astrobiology.

Include a zoomable scale-of-the-universe visualisation that starts with familiar objects and expands from microscopic scales to the Solar System, galaxy and observable universe. Make it clear that the scale and position of objects may be illustrative.

Add a gravity playground that compares the weight of an object on Earth, the Moon, Mars, Jupiter and other celestial bodies. Distinguish mass from weight, explain gravitational acceleration and illustrate orbital motion.

THE ROCKET AND SPACECRAFT ENGINEERING LAB:
Create a dedicated rocket engineering environment where users can design, customise and virtually launch spacecraft.

Let users choose a rocket configuration, adjust its mass, payload, fuel allocation and engine characteristics within an educational model, then run a simulated launch and examine the results.

Display:
- Thrust and weight.
- Gravity and gravitational acceleration.
- Aerodynamic drag.
- Propellant mass and changing vehicle mass.
- Thrust-to-weight ratio.
- Acceleration during flight.
- Velocity and altitude over time.
- Atmospheric pressure changes.
- Staging and the purpose of multistage vehicles.
- Orbital insertion and orbital velocity.
- Escape velocity.
- Payload capacity and trade-offs.
- Energy consumption and efficiency.
- Re-entry heating and thermal protection.
- Parachutes, landings and recovery systems.
- Satellite deployment.
- Reusable launch vehicles.
- Robotic spacecraft and planetary landers.

Build a rocket configuration screen with selectable visual components such as a nose cone, payload section, tanks, engine, fairing and stages. Give each component a description of its purpose. Use an illustrative model rather than claiming to provide real-world certified rocket designs.

Create a launch sequence with a countdown, camera changes, engine ignition effects, sound that can be muted, atmospheric ascent, stage separation and a flight telemetry panel. Let users replay the launch in slow motion, pause it and inspect the data.

Include a rocket comparison tool covering different propulsion concepts: chemical rockets, solid motors, liquid-propellant engines, ion propulsion, electric propulsion, nuclear thermal concepts and solar sails. Explain trade-offs involving thrust, efficiency, fuel type and mission duration.

Do not give operational instructions for building dangerous propulsion systems, handling energetic materials or constructing real rockets. Keep the experience focused on physics, systems engineering, historical context and educational simulations.

THE COMBUSTION AND ENERGY LAB:
Create a dedicated energy laboratory explaining combustion, fuels, heat, flames, energy transfer and the science of engines.

Include:
- What combustion is.
- The role of fuel, an oxidiser and ignition.
- Exothermic reactions.
- Activation energy.
- Heat transfer by conduction, convection and radiation.
- Complete and incomplete combustion.
- Why different fuels release different amounts of energy.
- Chemical energy, thermal energy and mechanical energy.
- Internal combustion engines.
- Turbines and jet engines.
- Steam engines and heat engines.
- Electricity generation.
- Energy efficiency and waste heat.
- Fossil fuels and their environmental effects.
- Hydrogen as an energy carrier.
- Batteries and energy storage.
- Solar, wind, hydroelectric and geothermal power.
- Nuclear fission and fusion at a conceptual level.
- Fire safety, smoke, carbon monoxide and combustion hazards.

Build an interactive combustion triangle showing the three requirements commonly taught for sustaining fire: fuel, heat and an oxidising agent. Let users explore how removing one requirement can stop combustion in a conceptual, non-operational simulation.

Create a heat-transfer simulator with temperature graphs and particles moving at different speeds. Explain how energy moves between warmer and cooler regions and why temperature is not the same as total thermal energy.

Add a virtual engine demonstration showing how chemical energy can become heat and then mechanical work. Compare the general operating principles of piston engines, turbines and electric motors.

Ensure the section clearly distinguishes combustion from detonation, and from nuclear reactions. Do not provide dangerous ignition methods, homemade explosive formulas, incendiary instructions or procedures for generating fires. Focus on safe educational explanations and hazard awareness.

ELECTRICITY AND MAGNETISM LAB:
Add interactive experiments covering electric charges, current, voltage, resistance, circuits, batteries, magnets, coils, motors and generators.

Allow users to assemble simple virtual circuits from a battery, wires, switches, resistors, bulbs and meters. The circuit must react to changes made by the user.

Display current, voltage and resistance, and introduce Ohm’s law. Visualise electron flow using a simplified model without confusing electron drift with the propagation of electrical signals.

Include series and parallel circuits, electromagnets, magnetic fields, induction, electric motors, generators, transformers and renewable electricity systems.

Add an electrical safety area explaining why mains electricity and high-voltage equipment can be dangerous. Keep the build activities virtual and avoid instructions for unsafe electrical work.

ATOMS, PARTICLES AND QUANTUM SCIENCE:
Explain matter at progressively deeper scales: everyday objects, molecules, atoms, nuclei, protons, neutrons, electrons, quarks and other fundamental particles.

Include interactive diagrams for atomic structure, isotopes, ions, the periodic table and radioactive decay. Explain how quantum mechanics describes physical systems at small scales, using approachable visualisations of probability, quantised energy levels, wave-particle duality and quantum tunnelling.

Clearly identify where an animation or analogy simplifies the real science. Do not present classical planetary electron orbits as an accurate literal description of atomic structure.

Include particle accelerators, antimatter, the Standard Model and open questions in modern physics. Explain the difference between established observations, scientific hypotheses and unresolved questions.

BIOLOGY AND THE HUMAN BODY:
Expand the website beyond physics and chemistry with a biology lab covering cells, DNA, genetics, evolution, ecosystems and human physiology.

Include interactive cell diagrams, DNA models, food webs, natural selection simulations and visual explanations of how the respiratory, circulatory, nervous and digestive systems work.

Explain photosynthesis, cellular respiration, microorganisms, immunity, biodiversity, climate impacts on ecosystems and the scientific method used in biological research.

Use accessible educational language, medically responsible explanations and carefully sourced facts.

EARTH SCIENCE AND NATURAL PHENOMENA:
Add a dynamic Earth science section covering the structure of the planet, tectonic plates, earthquakes, volcanoes, mountain formation, erosion, minerals, rocks, the carbon cycle, soil, oceans and the atmosphere.

Create a virtual tectonic plate demonstration where users observe how plates collide, separate and slide past one another. Include earthquake-wave visualisations and explain why their effects depend on magnitude, depth, distance, geology and building conditions.

Add a volcano simulator using an educational model to show magma movement, pressure and the role of viscosity and gas content. Keep it scientifically grounded and clearly distinguish a simplified model from real volcanic forecasting.

MACHINES, ROBOTICS AND ENGINEERING:
Create a practical engineering playground for simple machines and mechanical systems.

Include levers, pulleys, gears, inclined planes, wheels, axles, springs, hydraulic systems, pneumatic systems, robotic arms, sensors and control systems.

Allow users to assemble simple mechanisms and observe how forces, mechanical advantage and motion change when components are rearranged.

Add an engineering challenge mode in which users design a bridge, build a machine, optimise a wind turbine or design a robot to complete a task under specified constraints.

Provide a clear explanation of the trade-offs in each challenge, including mass, strength, stability, energy consumption and efficiency.

SOUND, LIGHT AND WAVES:
Create a wave laboratory covering sound waves, light waves, frequency, wavelength, amplitude, resonance, reflection, refraction, diffraction, interference and standing waves.

Allow users to change frequency and amplitude and see an animated waveform react in real time. Include comparisons between sound travelling through different media and light travelling through materials.

Explain why sound cannot propagate through a vacuum, how echoes work, how lenses form images, why the sky appears blue and how the electromagnetic spectrum extends from radio waves to gamma rays.

Add a virtual optics table with mirrors, lenses, light sources and a detector. Provide simplified ray diagrams and explain their assumptions.

INTERACTIVE SCIENCE SIMULATION SYSTEM:
Create a shared simulation framework throughout the website. Simulations should support appropriate controls such as play, pause, reset, step forward, speed adjustment, variable sliders, dropdown selectors, numeric inputs and visible outputs.

When a user changes a parameter, update the simulation where possible. Show graphs, measurements, units, a short explanation and relevant equations alongside the visualisation.

Include an optional “Show equations” setting and a “Explain this like I am a beginner” button. Also provide a deeper technical explanation for advanced users.

For suitable deterministic models, display the parameters and equations being used. Clearly label approximations, omitted physical effects and uncertainties. Do not claim that every simulation uses a full scientific solver.

Where realistic 3D simulation is impractical, build excellent 2D or interactive schematic alternatives instead of creating fake controls or misleading animations.

VIRTUAL LAB NOTEBOOK:
Every experiment should have an optional lab notebook where users can record their question, hypothesis, variables, observations, data and conclusion.

Include tables and graphs for experimental results, a clear distinction between independent, dependent and controlled variables, and the ability to repeat the same experiment with different settings.

Allow users to save their own observations and export experiment notes as a printable report when the platform supports it. If accounts and persistent storage are available, provide a private saved-project area.

SCIENCE CHALLENGES AND GAME MODES:
Build an interactive science challenge section with short missions, puzzles and longer engineering challenges.

Examples:
- Match elements to their symbols.
- Balance a chemical equation.
- Predict whether a familiar substance dissolves in water.
- Identify the products of a verified, supported introductory reaction.
- Calculate the speed of a moving object.
- Find the acceleration from a graph.
- Predict whether an object floats or sinks.
- Build a stable virtual bridge.
- Guide a satellite into an orbit.
- Design a virtual rocket to carry a payload.
- Improve the efficiency of a wind turbine.
- Identify the requirements of combustion.
- Trace energy through a power station.
- Classify stars and planets.
- Discover which circuit will illuminate a bulb.
- Explain why a ship floats.
- Solve a problem using Newton's laws.

Use points, progress indicators, optional achievements, difficulty levels and explanations after each answer. Reward correct reasoning and improvement rather than simply making science competitive.

Provide three difficulty modes:
Beginner — straightforward language, visual demonstrations and guided steps.
Intermediate — formulas, unit conversions, scientific explanations and independent experimentation.
Advanced — more technical equations, model assumptions, graphs, quantitative analysis and engineering trade-offs.

Do not make incorrect answers embarrassing. Explain the reasoning and let users try again.

SCIENCE KNOWLEDGE BASE:
Build a structured, searchable library of science topics with short introductions, detailed explanations, important vocabulary, key equations, practical examples, diagrams, references and connections to other topics.

Every article should include:
- A clear title and summary.
- A beginner-friendly explanation.
- An optional deeper explanation.
- Relevant diagrams or illustrations.
- Equations with definitions of every variable and its units.
- Real-world applications.
- Common misconceptions.
- A short knowledge check.
- Related subjects.
- Sources or further reading when appropriate.

Include a searchable glossary of scientific terminology. Users should be able to search for a word such as “combustion”, “momentum”, “molecule”, “orbital velocity” or “pressure” and receive a clear definition, examples and related simulations.

SCIENCE AI ASSISTANT:
Add an optional built-in AI science tutor if an AI model can be connected to the website.

The tutor should help users understand scientific concepts, interpret experiment data, explain equations, compare competing theories, generate practice questions and guide learners through problems step by step.

Users should be able to select a subject and preferred explanation level before asking a question. The tutor should support follow-up questions and link answers to relevant articles and simulations inside the website.

The tutor must prioritise scientific accuracy, state uncertainty honestly, distinguish established knowledge from speculation and avoid fabricating citations, research papers, equations or experimental results.

For potentially hazardous chemical reactions, fires, explosives, dangerous electrical setups or unsafe propulsion activities, the tutor should give safe conceptual explanations rather than actionable harmful instructions.

Do not present an AI assistant as connected or functional until the required model, credentials, backend and security measures have actually been implemented.

SEARCH AND DISCOVERY:
Create a fast global search that searches articles, elements, glossary entries, experiments, simulations, spacecraft, planets and challenges. Show relevant results with category labels and short descriptions.

Include filters for subject, difficulty, content type and estimated learning time. Add a discovery page that helps users find new science topics and see connections between disciplines.

