MY LEARNING:
Provide a learner dashboard with recently opened content, saved articles, completed lessons, challenge results and learning-path progress.

If accounts are implemented, support saved projects and private notes securely. Clearly distinguish local progress from account-synchronised progress. Do not claim data has been saved persistently unless the appropriate storage is implemented.

DESIGN OF SCIENCE PAGES:
Each major topic should have a consistent layout:
A visually distinctive introduction, a featured interactive simulation, an explanation of the science, key equations or principles, related experiments, a knowledge check and links to related subjects.

Avoid presenting each page as a wall of text. Use a combination of diagrams, interactive visualisations, short sections, informative cards, graphs, tooltips and expandable explanations. Keep scientific detail available without overwhelming beginners.

Add thoughtful micro-interactions, loading states, empty states, helpful error messages, keyboard-accessible controls and reduced-motion support. Ensure animations do not interfere with reading.

VISUAL EFFECTS AND ASSETS:
Use high-quality, scientifically appropriate illustrations of molecules, planetary systems, rockets, engines, atmospheric circulation, waves, fluids, atoms and machinery.

Use CSS animations, SVG, Canvas or WebGL for interactions where appropriate. A 3D library such as Three.js may be used if the development environment supports it. Avoid heavy effects on mobile devices.

Visual effects must help explain the science. For example, a temperature increase may be shown through particle motion, gravity through orbital paths, wind through airflow particles, and chemical reactions through molecular diagrams.

Do not make scientific diagrams unnecessarily decorative at the expense of clarity. Clearly label schematic and not-to-scale illustrations.

ACCESSIBILITY, PERFORMANCE AND MOBILE:
Use semantic HTML, keyboard navigation, visible focus indicators, accessible form labels, readable typography and appropriate contrast. Do not rely on colour alone to explain results.

Provide alternative text for informative images, transcripts for educational audio or video and accessible alternatives for interactive graphics.

Use efficient asset loading, compress images, lazy-load appropriate media and prevent simulations from consuming unnecessary resources. Make touch controls large enough for mobile users. Provide fallback visualisations when 3D or advanced rendering is unavailable.

TECHNICAL IMPLEMENTATION:
Use the technology supported by the chosen website builder. If generating a custom application, use a component-based architecture, reusable UI elements, clear state management and well-organised modules for each science subject.

Build working pages and genuine interactions rather than a collection of static mockups.

Implement:
- Reusable navigation and layout.
- A central science-topic data structure.
- Search and filters.
- Interactive lesson components.
- Reusable graph and simulation controls.
- Unit-aware calculations.
- Input validation and helpful error handling.
- Loading, empty and error states.
- Responsive styling.
- Appropriate metadata and page titles.
- Reliable navigation and direct page links.
- Secure integration for any optional AI services.

Keep private API keys on a secure server side. Do not expose secret keys inside frontend JavaScript. If accounts are added, use secure authentication and appropriate data-access rules.

Do not create nonfunctional login forms, fake save buttons, invented live data feeds or simulated AI responses presented as real service integrations. Clearly identify features that require an external service.

SCIENTIFIC ACCURACY AND TRUST:
Use established scientific knowledge and dependable educational references. Prefer recognised scientific institutions, textbooks, academic resources and authoritative space, weather, chemistry and physics sources.

For changing facts, use dated references and update information when reliable current sources are available. Explain uncertainty, measurement limitations and differences between idealised models and real-world behaviour.

Do not invent exact reaction outcomes, chemical products, rocket performance, planetary statistics, experimental measurements or scientific consensus.

Include a clear safety and educational-use policy. The virtual laboratory is for understanding science, not for replacing laboratory supervision, professional engineering or safety procedures.

