FINAL QUALITY REQUIREMENTS:
The website should feel like a complete, premium interactive science platform that people genuinely want to explore for hours. It should balance impressive presentation with accurate explanations, meaningful experimentation and useful educational content.

Prioritise, in this order:
1. A polished and functional homepage.
2. Reliable global navigation and search.
3. A genuinely interactive chemistry mixing simulator with safe, verified examples.
4. A working physics and speed laboratory.
5. Water, wind and fluid dynamics simulations.
6. An immersive Solar System explorer.
7. A functional virtual rocket engineering and launch simulator.
8. A combustion and energy laboratory.
9. Interactive electricity, biology, Earth science and engineering modules.
10. Quizzes, learning tracks, a virtual lab notebook and optional AI tutoring.

Test every major button, navigation item, selector, slider, form and simulation. Remove broken links, console errors and obvious layout issues. Ensure that each main section has meaningful content and at least one useful interaction where appropriate.

If a complex feature cannot be fully implemented, create a clearly labelled, functional educational version instead of a fake advanced simulation.

The finished result must be visually stunning, educationally valuable, scientifically responsible, accessible, responsive, fast and genuinely interactive.

Make SciVerse feel like a place where someone can explore a chemical reaction, understand why an object accelerates, watch wind move around an obstacle, investigate the planets, design a virtual spacecraft and discover the fundamental laws connecting everything in the universe — all from one website.